<DocsLayout
  title="Auth, roles & permissions"
  path="/docs/system/auth-authorization"
  description="Guards, multi-factor authentication, passkeys, the role hierarchy, and how authorization is enforced."
>
  <p>
    KoAkademy handles sensitive student data, so authentication and
    authorization are layered. This page covers the guards, the sign-in
    hardening options, the role system, and where checks are enforced.
  </p>

  <h2>Guards and audiences</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Guard</th>
        <th>Used by</th>
        <th>Notes</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>web</code></td>
        <td>Admin panel, admin workspace, faculty/student portals</td>
        <td>Session-based; portals additionally check role middleware</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>portal</code></td>
        <td>The stub Filament <code>portal</code> panel</td>
        <td>Exists for completeness; real portals use <code>web</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sanctum</code></td>
        <td><code>/api/*</code></td>
        <td>Bearer tokens issued per deployment</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Sign-in options: email/password, <strong>Socialite</strong> social providers (configurable
    per installation), <strong>passkeys</strong> (WebAuthn — public login options/verify
    endpoints), and a demo-login route used for evaluation. Signup supports email
    lookup with OTP verification, and there is a faculty-verification flow for staff
    accounts.
  </p>

  <h2>Multi-factor authentication</h2>
  <p>
    Two second factors ship in the box, implemented as custom Filament
    authentication responses:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>TOTP authenticator apps</strong> — recoverable app-based codes (<code
        >SecurityAwareAppAuthentication</code
      >).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Email codes</strong> — 30-minute one-time codes (<code
        >SecurityAwareEmailAuthentication</code
      >).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Users manage 2FA, passkeys, active sessions, and personal API keys from
    their profile settings — in the admin panel (Edit Profile) and in the React
    portals alike. A dedicated 2FA challenge route handles the post-login step.
  </p>

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <p>
    Roles come from <strong>spatie/laravel-permission</strong> with
    <strong>Filament Shield</strong> generating and enforcing per-resource permissions
    in the admin panel.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>super_admin</code> bypasses checks and can cross school boundaries.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <code>UserRole</code> enum defines roughly <strong
        >30 institution roles</strong
      > — developer, super admin, admin, president, registrar, cashier, librarian,
      support staff, professor ranks, student, graduate, SHS student, and more.
    </li>
    <li>
      The enum carries <strong>hierarchy levels</strong>,
      <code>getManageableRoles()</code> (which roles a given role may assign), and
      portal-access helpers — use these instead of hard-coding role names.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Typical permission shapes in the panel are Shield-generated (<code
      >view_any_student</code
    >, <code>update_student</code>, …); outside the panel, per-model <strong
      >policies</strong
    > authorize actions for both app and module models.
  </p>

  <h2>Where authorization is enforced</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Middleware</strong> — first gate at the route level:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>role:</code> (spatie) and custom <code>administrators.only</code
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>EnsureAdministrativePortalAccess</code>, <code
            >EnsureFacultyAccess</code
          >, <code>EnsureStudentAccess</code> — keep each audience in its own portal
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>EnsureFeatureEnabled</code> — blocks disabled features (see Modules
          &amp; feature flags)
        </li>
        <li><code>EnsureSystemIsSetup</code> — guards the first-run wizard</li>
        <li>
          <code>SetTenantContext</code> — establishes the school scope (see Architecture
          &amp; domain model)
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Policies</strong> — model-level authorization in controllers, Filament
      resources, and API handlers. Filament resources defer to the same policies,
      so a rule change applies everywhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Form requests / controllers</strong> — validate input and call
      <code>authorize</code> for protected actions; never trust portal-side hiding
      as a security boundary.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Audit and impersonation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Activity logging</strong> (spatie/laravel-activitylog) records administrative
      changes; the admin workspace exposes them under
      <strong>Audit Logs</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Impersonation</strong> (stechstudio/filament-impersonate) lets privileged
      staff sign in as another user for support, with an explicit stop-impersonation
      route. Treat impersonation as an audited, permission-gated action.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Contributing rules of thumb</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      New protected action → new or existing <strong>policy</strong> ability, enforced
      in the controller/resource — not just hidden UI.
    </li>
    <li>
      New portal route → attach the <strong>audience middleware</strong> for that
      portal.
    </li>
    <li>
      Never bypass the school scope; super-admin-only features should check the
      role explicitly.
    </li>
    <li>Tokens, codes, and secrets never appear in logs, URLs, or source.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
